Paste this paragraph into the machine's CLAUDE.md (or AGENTS.md). It is the
half of `filesearch/` that an agent has to be told about: the index exists,
but nothing uses it unless the instructions say to.

---

**Finding a file by name across `$HOME` is `plocate`, never `find ~`.**
`plocate -d ~/.cache/plocate/home.db -i '<pattern>'` answers in milliseconds
from an index of the home directory that a user timer
(`airlock-filesearch.timer`) rebuilds hourly; it skips `.git`,
`node_modules`, `.pnpm-store`, `__pycache__` and `.venv`. A file created in
the last hour may be missing: run
`systemctl --user start airlock-filesearch.service` (about 16 s on a
150 GB disk) and ask again. Inside one repository use `fd`, which walks the
tree live and needs no index.

Under WSL, `$HOME` and `/mnt/<drive>` are two different filesystems with two
different indexes. `plocate` still covers `$HOME` on the Linux side. It never
covers the Windows host, so a search under `/mnt/c/...` or any other mounted
drive needs `es`, voidtools Everything's client, reached under its bare name
on PATH. A file not turning up in a plocate search of `$HOME` is not evidence
that the file does not exist; it may simply be on the other side, under
`/mnt/<drive>`, where only `es` can find it.
